  <skos:Concept rdf:about="a82d885e-34cd-496a-b34d-17a23ad04126">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="57383ac5-614c-4b84-9202-e137b000422b"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SOLAR ENERGETIC PARTICLE PROPERTIES</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2024-09-23 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The properties of solar energetic particles, which are high-energy particles produced by the Sun. They can be emitted during solar flares or coronal mass ejections and can be a source of radiation that reaches the Earth. Events involving solar particles have varying occurrence times, magnitudes, durations, and compositions. The peak radiation lasts hours and can last anywhere from a few days to a week. Only a tiny percentage of directed solar flares and coronal mass ejections strike the Earth. Several operational problems with spacecraft may have been caused by certain key occurrences connected to previous solar cycles.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mseb.2023.117096" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="0b2ca4d1-a225-4243-90eb-1b482fb094a5"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="36bab763-b5d7-450a-8328-1c1f935184f4"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="630c1f3f-73b9-4f80-bc47-4cbf1fa43788"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="68f1ff0e-2e23-4025-ba71-7f6177352311"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="fd42b8e3-b76c-4888-aeb7-e6486beb4b69"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="bc02662f-d4a1-43c6-833f-836107ae6737"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="c39210ba-7659-424b-a2f0-5777a1519115"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The properties of solar energetic particles, which are high-energy particles produced by the Sun. They can be emitted during solar flares or coronal mass ejections and can be a source of radiation that reaches the Earth. Events involving solar particles have varying occurrence times, magnitudes, durations, and compositions. The peak radiation lasts hours and can last anywhere from a few days to a week. Only a tiny percentage of directed solar flares and coronal mass ejections strike the Earth. Several operational problems with spacecraft may have been caused by certain key occurrences connected to previous solar cycles.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2024-09-23 10:06:42.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="ed49aea3-c1ce-4522-985b-1b1b2b2c7790">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="09d1435d-99de-4149-ba64-d98c3335a383"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">GEOMAGNETIC STORM</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Space Studies Board, Commission on Physical Sciences, Mathematics, and Applications National Research Council &apos;http://www.nas.edu/ssb/glossary.html&apos;  &apos;http://www.geolab.nrcan.gc.ca/geomag/e_effects.html&apos;" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Any type of rapidly varying perturbation to Earth&apos;s magnetic field caused byelectric current flowing in the magnetosphere and ionosphere. Geomagnetic StormAdvisory will incorporate information on geomagnetic storms that may interferewith some technology, including satellite communications.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="fbec5145-79e6-4ed0-a804-6228aa6daba5">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="e9f67a66-e9fc-435c-b720-ae32a2c3d8f5"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">BIOLOGICAL CLASSIFICATION</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2017-03-15 User Id=mgenazzi Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Definition added
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Biological classification is the process by which scientists group living organisms. Organisms are classified based on how similar they are. Historically, similarity was determined by examining the physical characteristics of an organism but modern classification uses a variety of techniques including genetic analysis.
Organisms are classified according to a system of seven ranks:

Kingdom
Phylum
Class
Order
Family
Genus
Species
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="www.amentsoc.org" xml:lang="en"/>
