  <skos:Concept rdf:about="ab7eb13f-5fcb-4afa-8819-c37d36feeec1">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="0a454dc9-de56-4682-8688-36ffd547d42f"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">COCCOLITHOPHORES</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2017-03-15 User Id=mgenazzi Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Definition added
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Coccolithophores are extremely small, flagellated organisms of the KingdomProtista, which are adorned with buttons of calcium carbonate, called coccoliths.  The coccolithophores appear to contribute significantly to marine food chains by capturing radiant energy and manufacturing food during photosynthesis. The coccolithophores make up a considerable portion of the microscopic life in warmer regions, such as the Sargasso Sea.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2024-11-05 User Id=tstevens Entity=WeightedRelation Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert WeightedRelation
New Value=id: null
related concept uuid: e88cc54b-7a4b-4680-b441-4d10a4534cd9
relationship type: null
relationship value: null
generated by: null</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Lerman, Matthew.  1986.  Marine Biology.  The Benjamin Cummings Publishing Company.  Menlo Park, California.  KMS" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Coccolithophores are extremely small, flagellated organisms of the KingdomProtista, which are adorned with buttons of calcium carbonate, called coccoliths.  The coccolithophores appear to contribute significantly to marine food chains by capturing radiant energy and manufacturing food during photosynthesis. The coccolithophores make up a considerable portion of the microscopic life in warmer regions, such as the Sargasso Sea.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2024-11-05 10:55:33.0</dcterms:modified>
