  <skos:Concept rdf:about="d11df264-e70d-456c-9223-07f34e80b352">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="112e71ec-c0a1-49a8-82d7-bcb317b45860"/>
    <dcterms:created>2012-06-26</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">TREE LINE SHIFT</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-18 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The tree line is the edge of the habitat at which trees are capable of growing. Beyond the tree line, they are unable to grow because of inappropriate environmental conditions (usually cold temperatures or lack of moisture).[1]:51 Some distinguish additionally a deeper timberline or forest line, where trees form a forest with a closed canopy.[2]:151

At the tree line, tree growth is often very stunted, with the last trees forming low, densely matted bushes. If it is caused by wind, it is known as krummholz formation, from the German for &apos;twisted wood&apos;.[3]:58

The tree line, like many other natural lines (lake boundaries, for example), appears well-defined from a distance, but upon sufficiently close inspection, it is a gradual transition in most places. Trees grow shorter towards the inhospitable climate until they simply stop growing.[3]:55
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-26 User Id=gee-cee Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=TREE LINE SHIFT [d11df264-e70d-456c-9223-07f34e80b352,39865] - LAND SURFACE/AGRICULTURE INDICATORS [112e71ec-c0a1-49a8-82d7-bcb317b45860,39835]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Wikipedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tree_line 1) Elliott-Fisk, D.L. (2000). &apos;The Taiga and Boreal Forest&apos;. In Barbour, M.G.; Billings, M.D. (2nd ed.). Cambridge University Press. ISBN 978-0-521-55986-7. 2) Jørgensen, S.E. (2009). Ecosystem Ecology. Academic Press. ISBN 978-0-444-53466-8. 3) Zwinger, A.; Willard, B. E. (1996). Land Above the Trees: A Guide to American Alpine Tundra. Big Earth Publishing. ISBN 1-55566-171-8." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The tree line is the edge of the habitat at which trees are capable of growing. Beyond the tree line, they are unable to grow because of inappropriate environmental conditions (usually cold temperatures or lack of moisture).[1]:51 Some distinguish additionally a deeper timberline or forest line, where trees form a forest with a closed canopy.[2]:151At the tree line, tree growth is often very stunted, with the last trees forming low, densely matted bushes. If it is caused by wind, it is known as krummholz formation, from the German for &apos;twisted wood&apos;.[3]:58The tree line, like many other natural lines (lake boundaries, for example), appears well-defined from a distance, but upon sufficiently close inspection, it is a gradual transition in most places. Trees grow shorter towards the inhospitable climate until they simply stop growing.[3]:55</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-07-18 18:39:00.0</dcterms:modified>
