    <skos:broader rdf:resource="19ab681c-bdd7-4793-bbdb-1ec498575314"/>
    <dcterms:created>2015-07-07</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">FORMALDEHYDE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2015-07-07 User Id=gee-cee Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=FORMALDEHYDE [bc05d7d2-3c96-4bb6-b759-d45e3c673b86,158073] - CARBON AND HYDROCARBON COMPOUNDS [19ab681c-bdd7-4793-bbdb-1ec498575314,83945]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2017-03-15 User Id=sritz Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Formaldehyde is very soluble in water; solutions of formaldehyde (formalin) are used in biological labs to preserve samples. Formaldehyde is fairly toxic; health concerns are associated with its emission from foam, cavity insulation, or new plastic materials such as upholstery, carpet, etc. Formaldehyde is present in the atmosphere as an intermediate in the oxidation of methane and many other hydrocarbons; its photolysis is a major source of free radicals in the atmosphere.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Glossary of Meteorology, American Meteorological Society, 2017." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Formaldehyde is very soluble in water; solutions of formaldehyde (formalin) are used in biological labs to preserve samples. Formaldehyde is fairly toxic; health concerns are associated with its emission from foam, cavity insulation, or new plastic materials such as upholstery, carpet, etc. Formaldehyde is present in the atmosphere as an intermediate in the oxidation of methane and many other hydrocarbons; its photolysis is a major source of free radicals in the atmosphere.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2017-03-15 17:20:05.0</dcterms:modified>
