    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">GRAVITY ANOMALIES</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-10-10 User Id=gee-cee Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The difference between the observed acceleration of Earth&apos;s gravity and a value predicted from a model.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-10-12 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
User Note=added definition
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=The difference between the observed acceleration of Earth&apos;s gravity and a value predicted from a model.
New Value=The Earth&apos;s gravity field is determined by how the material that makes up the Earth is distributed throughout the Earth. Because gravity changes over the surface of the Earth, the weight of an object changes along with it. One can define standard gravity as the value of gravity for a perfectly smooth &apos;idealized&apos; Earth, and the gravity &apos;anomaly&apos; is a measure of how actual gravity deviates from this standard. A map of gravity anomalies (usually expressed in units of milliGals) tends to highlight short wavelength features better than a map of the geoid.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-10-10 User Id=gee-cee Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=GRAVITY ANOMALIES [fb7eeee0-9ad1-40f8-baa2-df7dc3acb6d3,61293] - GEODETICS/GRAVITY [221386f6-ef9b-4990-82b3-f990b0fe39fa,21319]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-10-12 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
User Note=added definition
System Note=update Definition
New Value=The University of Texas at Austin (http://www.csr.utexas.edu/grace/gravity/gravity_definition.html)</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="The University of Texas at Austin (http://www.csr.utexas.edu/grace/gravity/gravity_definition.html)" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The Earth&apos;s gravity field is determined by how the material that makes up the Earth is distributed throughout the Earth. Because gravity changes over the surface of the Earth, the weight of an object changes along with it. One can define standard gravity as the value of gravity for a perfectly smooth &apos;idealized&apos; Earth, and the gravity &apos;anomaly&apos; is a measure of how actual gravity deviates from this standard. A map of gravity anomalies (usually expressed in units of milliGals) tends to highlight short wavelength features better than a map of the geoid.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-10-12 09:07:30.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
