    <skos:changeNote>Date=2024-10-24 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Paleomagnetism is the study of natural remnant magnetization of the Earth&apos;s
materials in order to determine the itensity and direction of the Earth&apos;s
magnetic field in the geologic past. Variations in the Earth&apos;s magnetic field
can be used as a means of stratigraphic correlation. Major reversals of the
Earth&apos;s magnetic field are well known and the record of these reversals in
sediments can be used as time markers or chronostratigraphic horizons.
New Value=Paleomagnetism is the study of natural remnant magnetization of the Earth&apos;s
materials in order to determine the itensity and direction of the Earth&apos;s
magnetic field in the geologic past. Variations in the Earth&apos;s magnetic field
can be used as a means of stratigraphic correlation. Major reversals of the
Earth&apos;s magnetic field are well known and the record of these reversals in
sediments can be used as time markers or chronostratigraphic horizons.</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Jackson, J.A., ed. 1997. Glossary of Geology, 4th edition. American Geological Institute. Bradley, R.S. 1999. Paleoclimatology: Reconstructing Climates of the Quaternary, 2nd edition. Harcourt Academic Press." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Paleomagnetism is the study of natural remnant magnetization of the Earth&apos;smaterials in order to determine the itensity and direction of the Earth&apos;smagnetic field in the geologic past. Variations in the Earth&apos;s magnetic fieldcan be used as a means of stratigraphic correlation. Major reversals of theEarth&apos;s magnetic field are well known and the record of these reversals insediments can be used as time markers or chronostratigraphic horizons.</skos:definition>
