  <skos:Concept rdf:about="9d7b0259-2d88-4e78-b2c2-131a02d05c15">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="3526afb8-0dc9-43c7-8ad4-f34f250a1e91"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SOIL SALINITY/SOIL SODICITY</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2017-03-10 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=The term salinity refers to the presence of the major dissolved
inorganic solutes, essentially Na, Mg, Ca, K, Cl, SO4, HCO3, and CO3, in
aqueous samples. As applied to soils, it refers to the soluble plus
readily dissolvable salts in the soil or, more usually, in an aqueous
extract of a soil sample.  Salinity is quantified in terms of the total
concentration (or, occasionally, the content) of such soluble salts.
New Value=The term salinity refers to the presence of the major dissolved inorganic solutes, essentially Na, Mg, Ca, K, Cl, SO4, HCO3, and CO3, in aqueous samples. As applied to soils, it refers to the soluble plus readily dissolvable salts in the soil or, more usually, in an aqueous extract of a soil sample.  Salinity is quantified in terms of the total concentration (or, occasionally, the content) of such soluble salts.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2017-03-10 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Soil Science Society of America:  Methods of Soil Analysis.  1996.
New Value=Soil Science Society of America:  Methods of Soil Analysis.  1996.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2024-09-30 User Id=tstevens Entity=WeightedRelation Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert WeightedRelation
New Value=id: null
related concept uuid: 3b54403e-25a1-43cc-97ac-7c14e73bda96
relationship type: null
relationship value: null
generated by: null</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Soil Science Society of America:  Methods of Soil Analysis.  1996." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The term salinity refers to the presence of the major dissolved inorganic solutes, essentially Na, Mg, Ca, K, Cl, SO4, HCO3, and CO3, in aqueous samples. As applied to soils, it refers to the soluble plus readily dissolvable salts in the soil or, more usually, in an aqueous extract of a soil sample.  Salinity is quantified in terms of the total concentration (or, occasionally, the content) of such soluble salts.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2024-09-30 11:05:51.0</dcterms:modified>
