  <skos:Concept rdf:about="b1d51b72-97d0-484c-b251-220f219965c2">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="3375096a-7782-42e8-97d2-0febf63893e0"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-02-14</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">MARINE STRATOCUMULUS</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-03-21 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: As the Earth spins on its axis, the movement pushes ocean surface waters west away from the western edge of continents. In a process called upwelling, cool water from deep in the ocean rises to replace the surface water. Upwelling creates a layer of cool water at the surface, which cools the air immediately above the water. As the moist, marine air cools, water vapor condenses into water droplets, and low clouds form. These lumpy, sheet-like clouds are marine stratocumulus clouds, and they are a common occurrence along the western coasts of the continents, where upwelling is common. Probably no higher than a kilometer (about 3,000 feet) above the Earth’s surface, the clouds in the image hug the coastline in echo of the cool ocean currents beneath them. In contrast, warm, dry air dominates over land, keeping skies cloud free.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-02-14 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=MARINE STRATOCUMULUS [b1d51b72-97d0-484c-b251-220f219965c2,82233] - STRATOCUMULUS [3375096a-7782-42e8-97d2-0febf63893e0,82231]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="NASA (http://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/IOTD/view.php?id=7007)" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">As the Earth spins on its axis, the movement pushes ocean surface waters west away from the western edge of continents. In a process called upwelling, cool water from deep in the ocean rises to replace the surface water. Upwelling creates a layer of cool water at the surface, which cools the air immediately above the water. As the moist, marine air cools, water vapor condenses into water droplets, and low clouds form. These lumpy, sheet-like clouds are marine stratocumulus clouds, and they are a common occurrence along the western coasts of the continents, where upwelling is common. Probably no higher than a kilometer (about 3,000 feet) above the Earth’s surface, the clouds in the image hug the coastline in echo of the cool ocean currents beneath them. In contrast, warm, dry air dominates over land, keeping skies cloud free.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-03-21 13:35:41.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="ab1e152c-eab9-400a-a90f-15cb64ed2a75">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="346cade5-801a-4afc-9652-48d02905bc4f"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">VERTICAL WIND MOTION</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Glossary of Meteorology, American Meteorological Society, 1998." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The component of ocean wind motion rising perpendicular to the plane ofthe horizon.</skos:definition>
