    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">PARABOLIC DUNE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-06 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=PARABOLIC [db89fdd2-d911-4a75-9210-ce90db043358,105519] - DUNES [362993fc-743e-42bc-a011-459baea8f427,105509]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-20 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: U-shaped mounds of sand with convex noses trailed by elongated arms are parabolic dunes. Sometimes these dunes are called U-shaped, blowout, or hairpin dunes, and they are well known in coastal deserts. Unlike crescent shaped dunes, their crests point upwind. The elongated arms of parabolic dunes follow rather than lead because they have been fixed by vegetation, while the bulk of the sand in the dune migrates forward.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-20 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=PrefLabel Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update PrefLabel
Old Value=PARABOLIC
New Value=PARABOLIC DUNE</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="PhysicalGeography.net" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">U-shaped mounds of sand with convex noses trailed by elongated arms are parabolic dunes. Sometimes these dunes are called U-shaped, blowout, or hairpin dunes, and they are well known in coastal deserts. Unlike crescent shaped dunes, their crests point upwind. The elongated arms of parabolic dunes follow rather than lead because they have been fixed by vegetation, while the bulk of the sand in the dune migrates forward.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-08-20 08:57:07.0</dcterms:modified>
