    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">GREENHOUSE GASES</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2025-03-10 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=GREENHOUSE GASES [f03cac2e-6ee5-4734-9ab7-eb8521ba2654,2103180] - ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACTS [3f4cfc81-7745-43d9-b313-f68cdf72359b,2099764]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2025-03-10 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/topics/human-dimensions/industrial-emissions
New Value=Stephen H. Schneider, The Greenhouse Effect: Science and Policy. Science 243, 771-781 (1989). DOI: 10.1126/science.243.4892.771</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2025-03-10 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Greenhouse gases, including carbon dioxide, methane, and water vapor, absorb and re-emit thermal infrared radiation, thereby regulating Earth’s temperature and climate. While they are essential for maintaining habitable conditions by preventing heat loss into space, excessive emissions from human activities amplify this effect, contributing to climate change.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Stephen H. Schneider, The Greenhouse Effect: Science and Policy. Science 243, 771-781 (1989). DOI: 10.1126/science.243.4892.771" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Greenhouse gases, including carbon dioxide, methane, and water vapor, absorb and re-emit thermal infrared radiation, thereby regulating Earth’s temperature and climate. While they are essential for maintaining habitable conditions by preventing heat loss into space, excessive emissions from human activities amplify this effect, contributing to climate change.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2025-03-10 11:29:47.0</dcterms:modified>
