    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">PESTICIDES</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2018-03-06 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=PESTICIDES [bc1c6d8c-2e47-4a9f-aeb4-16b02bff4f19,310641] - CONTAMINANTS [42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7,84623]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2018-03-06 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Chemical compounds that are used to kill pests, including insects, rodents, fungi and unwanted plants (weeds). Pesticides are used in public health to kill vectors of disease, such as mosquitoes, and in agriculture, to kill pests that damage crops. By their nature, pesticides are potentially toxic to other organisms, including humans, and need to be used safely and disposed of properly.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="World Health Organization" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Chemical compounds that are used to kill pests, including insects, rodents, fungi and unwanted plants (weeds). Pesticides are used in public health to kill vectors of disease, such as mosquitoes, and in agriculture, to kill pests that damage crops. By their nature, pesticides are potentially toxic to other organisms, including humans, and need to be used safely and disposed of properly.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2018-03-06 13:34:35.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="ffc61516-ea33-48f3-94df-7065fea388ee">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="42424cb5-aa02-4e7e-b164-b2a3324285c6"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-03-05</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ELECTRICAL</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-03-22 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The electrical nature of a material is characterized by its conductivity (or, inversely, its resistivity) and its dielectric constant, and coefficients that indicate the rates of change of these with temperature, frequency at which measurement is made, and so on. For rocks with a range of chemical composition as well as variable physical properties of porosity and fluid content, the values of electrical properties can vary widely.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-03-05 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=ELECTRICAL [ffc61516-ea33-48f3-94df-7065fea388ee,83077] - NON-METALLIC MINERAL PHYSICAL/OPTICAL PROPERTIES [42424cb5-aa02-4e7e-b164-b2a3324285c6,83059]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Encyclopedia Britannica" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The electrical nature of a material is characterized by its conductivity (or, inversely, its resistivity) and its dielectric constant, and coefficients that indicate the rates of change of these with temperature, frequency at which measurement is made, and so on. For rocks with a range of chemical composition as well as variable physical properties of porosity and fluid content, the values of electrical properties can vary widely.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-03-22 08:19:55.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
