    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ZINC</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2018-03-07 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: A chemical element and the 24th most abundant element in Earth&apos;s crust and has five stable isotopes. The most common zinc ore is sphalerite (zinc blende), a zinc sulfide mineral. The largest workable lodes are in Australia, Asia, and the United States. Zinc is refined by froth flotation of the ore, roasting, and final extraction using electricity (electrowinning).
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2018-03-07 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=ZINC [ab12b3d6-2cbf-4a5a-a410-1d23afe906d8,310671] - CONTAMINANTS [42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7,84623]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Handbook of Chemistry and Physics" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A chemical element and the 24th most abundant element in Earth&apos;s crust and has five stable isotopes. The most common zinc ore is sphalerite (zinc blende), a zinc sulfide mineral. The largest workable lodes are in Australia, Asia, and the United States. Zinc is refined by froth flotation of the ore, roasting, and final extraction using electricity (electrowinning).</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2018-03-07 09:40:32.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
