  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="de7647c9-b129-4cba-afe4-63fa9998206e">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="4ad0c52d-6449-48ff-8678-adc6b2cebcb7"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SOLAR IRRADIANCE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2019-06-21 User Id=sritz Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add narrower relation
New Value=SOLAR IRRADIANCE [de7647c9-b129-4cba-afe4-63fa9998206e,85399] - SHORTWAVE DOWNWARD IRRADIANCE [e1af236f-ee88-4b10-8feb-70d9e09f90be,368891]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Maunder, W.J. 1994. Dictionary of Global Climate Change, 2nd. ed. Chapman and Hall, NY. Carleton, A.M. Satellite Remote Sensing in Climatology, Belhaven Press, London, UK." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="e1af236f-ee88-4b10-8feb-70d9e09f90be"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Solar irradiance at the top of the atmosphere on a plane normal to theincident radiation, and at the mean distance of the Earth from the Sun.Solar irradiance is also referred to as the solar constant. In satelliteremote sensing, the solar irradiance is used as an onboard calibrartion ofvisible band sensors. Some climate studies suggest that small variationsin the solar irradiance associated with solar activity over days todecades may have an effect the Earth&apos;s climate.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2019-06-21 11:04:49.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="dd316647-9043-40c3-9329-f22f9215fefa">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="4cc9b4fa-5097-447f-914c-eb90820938c6"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ATMOSPHERIC OZONE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add narrower relation
New Value=ATMOSPHERIC OZONE [dd316647-9043-40c3-9329-f22f9215fefa,84479] - TOTAL OZONE [b9107ec3-c777-4e71-9046-55bd7ed57ef0,560875]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add narrower relation
New Value=ATMOSPHERIC OZONE [dd316647-9043-40c3-9329-f22f9215fefa,84479] - OZONE SURFACE [e00bfcb8-8968-400d-af2e-86a288f3443f,560874]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add narrower relation
New Value=ATMOSPHERIC OZONE [dd316647-9043-40c3-9329-f22f9215fefa,84479] - OZONE PROFILES [959878c8-b02a-4bb8-ad10-96ae31ebe59f,560873]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Ozone (O3) is a molecule that consists of three oxygen atoms bonded
together. The ozone layer in the stratosphere absorbs UV radiation and
creates a warm layer of air high in the stratosphere. Ozone that is
present in the troposphere is mostly a result of anthropogenic pollution
and therefore higher concentrations are found in urban areas. Ozone is
involved with NOx in the photochemical production of many of the
constituents of pollution environments (see nitrogen oxides and hydroxyl
definitions) [Chemical and Engineering News; v72; 6-7; 1994.] [Aviation
Week and Space Technology; v140; 20-21; 1994.]
New Value=Atmospheric Ozone is one of the most important trace gases in our atmosphere that both benefits and harms life on Earth. High ground-level ozone amounts contribute to poor air quality, adversely affecting human health, agricultural productivity, and forested ecosystems. Ozone absorbs infrared radiation, and is most potent as a greenhouse gas in the cold upper troposphere located 8–15 km above the surface. In the stratosphere, between approximately 15 and 50 km above the Earth’s surface, a layer rich in ozone serves as a “sunscreen” for the world by shielding the Earth’s surface from harmful ultraviolet radiation. This absorption of solar energy also affects atmospheric circulation patterns and thus influences weather around the globe. Moreover, throughout the atmosphere, ozone is the key ingredient that initiates chemical cleansing of the atmosphere of various pollutants, such as carbon monoxide and methane, among others, which could otherwise accumulate to harmful levels or exert a stronger influence on climate. Therefore, changes to ozone anywhere in the atmosphere can have major impacts on the Earth.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=PrefLabel Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update PrefLabel
Old Value=OZONE
New Value=ATMOSPHERIC OZONE</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Atmospheric and Air Chemistry Glossary from the Sam Houston State University.
&quot;http://www.shsu.edu/~chemistry/Glossary/glos.html&quot;
New Value=https://www.ametsoc.org/index.cfm/ams/about-ams/ams-statements/statements-of-the-ams-in-force/atmospheric-ozone1/</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="https://www.ametsoc.org/index.cfm/ams/about-ams/ams-statements/statements-of-the-ams-in-force/atmospheric-ozone1/" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="959878c8-b02a-4bb8-ad10-96ae31ebe59f"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="b9107ec3-c777-4e71-9046-55bd7ed57ef0"/>
    <skos:narrower rdf:resource="e00bfcb8-8968-400d-af2e-86a288f3443f"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Atmospheric Ozone is one of the most important trace gases in our atmosphere that both benefits and harms life on Earth. High ground-level ozone amounts contribute to poor air quality, adversely affecting human health, agricultural productivity, and forested ecosystems. Ozone absorbs infrared radiation, and is most potent as a greenhouse gas in the cold upper troposphere located 8–15 km above the surface. In the stratosphere, between approximately 15 and 50 km above the Earth’s surface, a layer rich in ozone serves as a “sunscreen” for the world by shielding the Earth’s surface from harmful ultraviolet radiation. This absorption of solar energy also affects atmospheric circulation patterns and thus influences weather around the globe. Moreover, throughout the atmosphere, ozone is the key ingredient that initiates chemical cleansing of the atmosphere of various pollutants, such as carbon monoxide and methane, among others, which could otherwise accumulate to harmful levels or exert a stronger influence on climate. Therefore, changes to ozone anywhere in the atmosphere can have major impacts on the Earth.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2021-02-26 09:37:54.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
