    <dcterms:created>2012-06-26</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">FIRN LIMIT</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-26 User Id=gee-cee Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=FIRN LIMIT [9fec9f47-c45d-4f15-8be5-d71424f33647,39905] - CRYOSPHERIC INDICATORS [76b8c21c-c221-4724-86ef-c07222cb152b,39899]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-10 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: A line that marks the limit on a mountain above which snow persists from one winter to the next is called the annual snowline, and this line on a glacier is called the firnline. Above the firnline, snow that falls each year packs down and changes into glacier ice as air is slowly forced out of it. This part of the glacier is its accumulation area where more snow falls each year than is lost by melting. Below the firnline is the ablation area, where melting predominates.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="USGS - Glossary of Selected Glacier and Related Terminology - http://vulcan.wr.usgs.gov/Glossary/Glaciers/glacier_terminology.html" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A line that marks the limit on a mountain above which snow persists from one winter to the next is called the annual snowline, and this line on a glacier is called the firnline. Above the firnline, snow that falls each year packs down and changes into glacier ice as air is slowly forced out of it. This part of the glacier is its accumulation area where more snow falls each year than is lost by melting. Below the firnline is the ablation area, where melting predominates.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-07-10 10:38:34.0</dcterms:modified>
