    <dcterms:created>2025-05-22</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Lagrangian feature</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2025-05-22 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: 
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2025-05-22 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=The Lagrangian feature refers to a function that characterizes the state of a physical system. The Lagrangian feature is referenced in the Global multi-scale Eulerian and Lagrangian oceanic
2 environmental variables for the period 2000-2009. Daily near-global maps of two Lagrangian synoptic quantities (namely the finite-time Lyapunov exponent (FTLE) and dilation 78 rate) are computed here. Both of these variables are derived from the deformation tensor derived from 3-day gridded trajectories 79 computed from 3-hourly archived model velocities from the Hybrid Coordinate Ocean Model (HYCOM, see below) or OSCAR 80 (see below) The HYCOM archive is missing data for several times in the target decade. When the gap is less than 18 hours, 81 integration is performed with the existing data, linearly interpolating across the gap. For longer gaps, linear interpolation is 82 insufficient, and the fields for the affected dates are not computed. Hence, over the computed period, Lagrangian products 83 could not be computed with HYCOM for the period May 21 – 25, 2000.
New Value=The Lagrangian feature refers to a function that characterizes the state of a physical system. The Lagrangian feature is referenced in the Global multi-scale Eulerian and Lagrangian oceanic 2 environmental variables for the period 2000-2009. Daily near-global maps of two Lagrangian synoptic quantities (namely the finite-time Lyapunov exponent (FTLE) and dilation 78 rate) are computed here. Both of these variables are derived from the deformation tensor derived from 3-day gridded trajectories 79 computed from 3-hourly archived model velocities from the Hybrid Coordinate Ocean Model (HYCOM, see below) or OSCAR 80 (see below) The HYCOM archive is missing data for several times in the target decade. When the gap is less than 18 hours, 81 integration is performed with the existing data, linearly interpolating across the gap. For longer gaps, linear interpolation is 82 insufficient, and the fields for the affected dates are not computed. Hence, over the computed period, Lagrangian products 83 could not be computed with HYCOM for the period May 21 – 25, 2000.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2025-05-22 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
New Value=The Lagrangian feature refers to a function that characterizes the state of a physical system. The Lagrangian feature is referenced in the Global multi-scale Eulerian and Lagrangian oceanic
2 environmental variables for the period 2000-2009. Daily near-global maps of two Lagrangian synoptic quantities (namely the finite-time Lyapunov exponent (FTLE) and dilation 78 rate) are computed here. Both of these variables are derived from the deformation tensor derived from 3-day gridded trajectories 79 computed from 3-hourly archived model velocities from the Hybrid Coordinate Ocean Model (HYCOM, see below) or OSCAR 80 (see below) The HYCOM archive is missing data for several times in the target decade. When the gap is less than 18 hours, 81 integration is performed with the existing data, linearly interpolating across the gap. For longer gaps, linear interpolation is 82 insufficient, and the fields for the affected dates are not computed. Hence, over the computed period, Lagrangian products 83 could not be computed with HYCOM for the period May 21 – 25, 2000.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2025-05-22 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=Lagrangian feature [a110c0bd-0d41-4ac3-8463-16f97c57b266,2164039] - OCEAN CIRCULATION [a031952d-9f00-4ba5-9966-5f87ab9dfdd4,2160676]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="https://essopenarchive.org/users/798053/articles/1148596-global-multi-scale-eulerian-and-lagrangian-oceanic-environmental-variables-for-the-period-2000-2009" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The Lagrangian feature refers to a function that characterizes the state of a physical system. The Lagrangian feature is referenced in the Global multi-scale Eulerian and Lagrangian oceanic 2 environmental variables for the period 2000-2009. Daily near-global maps of two Lagrangian synoptic quantities (namely the finite-time Lyapunov exponent (FTLE) and dilation 78 rate) are computed here. Both of these variables are derived from the deformation tensor derived from 3-day gridded trajectories 79 computed from 3-hourly archived model velocities from the Hybrid Coordinate Ocean Model (HYCOM, see below) or OSCAR 80 (see below) The HYCOM archive is missing data for several times in the target decade. When the gap is less than 18 hours, 81 integration is performed with the existing data, linearly interpolating across the gap. For longer gaps, linear interpolation is 82 insufficient, and the fields for the affected dates are not computed. Hence, over the computed period, Lagrangian products 83 could not be computed with HYCOM for the period May 21 – 25, 2000.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2025-05-22 10:33:54.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="fc0a6bb2-27f0-48e8-89f1-ebfc7ccd4823">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="a031952d-9f00-4ba5-9966-5f87ab9dfdd4"/>
