    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ATMOSPHERIC NITRIC ACID</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Atmospheric and Air Chemistry Glossary from the Sam Houston State University.
&quot;http://www.shsu.edu/~chemistry/Glossary/glos.html&quot;
New Value=Atmospheric and Air Chemistry Glossary from the Sam Houston State University. &quot;http://www.shsu.edu/~chemistry/Glossary/glos.html&quot;</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Nitric Acid (HNO3) is a corrosive, non-volatile, inorganic acid. It is
formed by the conversion of nitrogen oxide into nitrogen dioxide, and then
into nitric acid, which is highly water soluble.  This solubility allows
easy removal of nitric acid from the troposphere into rain - commonly
referred to as acid rain.  Nitric acid has a relatively low concentration
in the atmosphere but provides an important role in the production of
sulfuric acid by acting as a catalyst in the conversion of sulfur dioxide
to sulfuric acid.   [T.E Graedel and Paul J.  Crutzen. Atmospheric Change:
An Earth System Perspective. WH Freeman and Co, NY.1993.]
New Value=Nitric Acid (HNO3) is a corrosive, non-volatile, inorganic acid. It is formed by the conversion of nitrogen oxide into nitrogen dioxide, and then into nitric acid, which is highly water soluble. This solubility allows easy removal of nitric acid from the troposphere into rain - commonly referred to as acid rain. Nitric acid has a relatively low concentration in the atmosphere but provides an important role in the production of
sulfuric acid by acting as a catalyst in the conversion of sulfur dioxide to sulfuric acid.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-03-02 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Atmospheric and Air Chemistry Glossary from the Sam Houston State University. &quot;http://www.shsu.edu/~chemistry/Glossary/glos.html&quot;
New Value=https://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Nitric_acid</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=PrefLabel Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update PrefLabel
Old Value=NITRIC ACID
New Value=ATMOSPHERIC NITRIC ACID</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-03-02 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Nitric Acid (HNO3) is a corrosive, non-volatile, inorganic acid. It is formed by the conversion of nitrogen oxide into nitrogen dioxide, and then into nitric acid, which is highly water soluble. This solubility allows easy removal of nitric acid from the troposphere into rain - commonly referred to as acid rain. Nitric acid has a relatively low concentration in the atmosphere but provides an important role in the production of
sulfuric acid by acting as a catalyst in the conversion of sulfur dioxide to sulfuric acid.
New Value=A very soluble, acidic gas, formula HNO3, the end product of the oxidation of emitted gases.

It is a major component of acidic precipitation in continental regions. In the clean background troposphere, its removal in precipitation acts as a sink for odd hydrogen and nitrogen compounds and limits the formation of ozone.</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="https://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Nitric_acid" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A very soluble, acidic gas, formula HNO3, the end product of the oxidation of emitted gases.It is a major component of acidic precipitation in continental regions. In the clean background troposphere, its removal in precipitation acts as a sink for odd hydrogen and nitrogen compounds and limits the formation of ozone.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2021-03-02 09:42:46.0</dcterms:modified>
