    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SALINE CONCENTRATION</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2018-03-14 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=reference 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=Fundamentals of Chemistry.
New Value=Fundamentals of Chemistry</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2018-03-07 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The concentration of dissolved salts in water, usually expressed in parts per thousand (permille, %) or parts per million (ppm). The United States Geological Survey classifies saline water in three salinity categories. Salt concentration in slightly saline water is around 1,000 to 3,000 ppm (0.1–0.3%), in moderately saline water 3,000 to 10,000 ppm (0.3–1%) and in highly saline water 10,000 to 35,000 ppm (1–3.5%). Seawater has a salinity of roughly 35,000 ppm, equivalent to 35 grams of salt per one liter (or kilogram) of water.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2018-03-07 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=SALINE CONCENTRATION [a38db528-3064-449d-ae70-af86997a11f4,310687] - WATER CHARACTERISTICS [f7e97dc3-1181-41b5-8b90-946eb2504110,310633]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Fundamentals of Chemistry" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The concentration of dissolved salts in water, usually expressed in parts per thousand (permille, %) or parts per million (ppm). The United States Geological Survey classifies saline water in three salinity categories. Salt concentration in slightly saline water is around 1,000 to 3,000 ppm (0.1–0.3%), in moderately saline water 3,000 to 10,000 ppm (0.3–1%) and in highly saline water 10,000 to 35,000 ppm (1–3.5%). Seawater has a salinity of roughly 35,000 ppm, equivalent to 35 grams of salt per one liter (or kilogram) of water.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2018-03-14 09:30:23.0</dcterms:modified>
