    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Palmer, W.C., 1965: &lt;i&gt;Meteorological Drought Research Paper 45&lt;/i&gt;, U.S. Weather Bureau, Washington, D.C. From: American Meterological Society, 2000: The Glossary of Meteorology, 2nd edition, Boston, MA See also &apos;http://www.drought.noaa.gov/palmer.html&apos;" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The Palmer Index or the Palmer Drought Severity Index (PDSI) is an index
formulated by Palmer (1965) and compares the actual amount of precipitation
received in an area during a specified period with the normal or average amountexpected during that same period.
&lt;P&gt;
The PDSI is based on a procedure of hydrologic or water balance accounting by
which excesses or deficiences in moisture are determined in relation to averageclimatic values.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="a0b5e8ee-e164-4713-97b7-a8e5100d5e9c">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="c69cde73-7bd9-489b-a20b-bd23cfb82d92"/>
    <dcterms:created>2020-05-07</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">STICKLEBACKS</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2020-05-07 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=STICKLEBACKS [a0b5e8ee-e164-4713-97b7-a8e5100d5e9c,560025] - RAY-FINNED FISHES [c69cde73-7bd9-489b-a20b-bd23cfb82d92,84365]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="https://www.fishbase.de/Summary/FamilySummary.php?Family=Gasterosteidae" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Distribution: Northern Hemisphere. Body may be elongate, naked or with bony scutes along the sides. Three to sixteen well-developed isolated dorsal spines preceed a normal dorsal fin having 6-14 rays; caudal fin rays 12-13; a single spine and 1-2 soft rays in the pelvic fin; three branchiostegal rays; circumorbital ring incomplete posteriorly; epineurals present; vertebrae 28-42. About 18 cm maximum length reached in Spinachia spinachia. Sticklebacks have been widely used in scientific studies. Several subspecies are recognized by authors and the family is in need of revision.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="a1451fce-9e69-4f2d-b2cf-27238a7577ce">
