  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="a090a598-3ae6-4fc4-b248-97ec5226702a">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="d2dc2330-0433-43f2-9154-dc399d24406c"/>
    <dcterms:created>2012-06-26</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">CHARCOAL SEDIMENT</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-26 User Id=gee-cee Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=CHARCOAL SEDIMENT [a090a598-3ae6-4fc4-b248-97ec5226702a,39993] - FIRE HISTORY [d2dc2330-0433-43f2-9154-dc399d24406c,39989]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-23 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Charcoal records from sediments can reconstruct much longer fires histories, but with less temporal and spatial precision than tree-ring records. Because charcoal particles can be carried aloft to great heights and transported great distances, the source of the charcoal may be from distant fires as well as local fires. Charcoal accumulation may continue for a few years after a fire because of transportation and redeposition of secondary charcoal. This process tends to blur the exact age of a fire, even when the charcoal particles are directly dated by radiocarbon dating, which itself has a dating precision of +/- 5%. Additionally, the charcoal deposited may represent more than one fire within the area, or fires from more than one year. As a result, fire episodes are referred to as one or more fires occurring in the time interval of interest, rather than individual fires.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="International Multiproxy Paleofire Database (IMDP) : NOAA/NCDC Paleoclimatology Program http://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/paleo/impd/paleofire.html" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Charcoal records from sediments can reconstruct much longer fires histories, but with less temporal and spatial precision than tree-ring records. Because charcoal particles can be carried aloft to great heights and transported great distances, the source of the charcoal may be from distant fires as well as local fires. Charcoal accumulation may continue for a few years after a fire because of transportation and redeposition of secondary charcoal. This process tends to blur the exact age of a fire, even when the charcoal particles are directly dated by radiocarbon dating, which itself has a dating precision of +/- 5%. Additionally, the charcoal deposited may represent more than one fire within the area, or fires from more than one year. As a result, fire episodes are referred to as one or more fires occurring in the time interval of interest, rather than individual fires.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-07-23 16:43:07.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="a56d397b-bff5-4a14-b54c-366470e023c7">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="d46a5046-e1c6-4a09-a2f1-db6a21eda611"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">CHLORINE DIOXIDE</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Atmospheric and Air Chemistry Glossary from the Sam Houston State University. &apos;http://www.shsu.edu/~chemistry/Glossary/glos.html&apos;" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">ClO2, a radical, undergoes photodecomposition in the stratosphere where
the products of this reaction react with ozone. Since this is a
photochemical reaction it only takes place while the sun is up.
Experiments over Antarctica have shown a direct relation between polar
ozone loss and the increase in halocarbon chemistry, which comes from
anthropogenic sources. Scientist are currently looking at the molecular
behavior of chlorine dioxide in the atmosphere in order to understand its
role in depletion of ozone more thoroughly. [Simon, J. D. and Vaida, V.
The Photoreactivity of Chlorine Dioxide. Science v 268; p. 1443-1448;
1995.]</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
