User Note=Move Concepts
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=ALKALINITY [a74059fe-6b15-4b55-8ea5-4a65b66c7e11,86125] - WATER CHARACTERISTICS [f7e97dc3-1181-41b5-8b90-946eb2504110,310633]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2018-03-07 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=DELETE 
User Note=Move Concepts
System Note=delete broader relation
Old Value=ALKALINITY [a74059fe-6b15-4b55-8ea5-4a65b66c7e11,86125] - WATER QUALITY/WATER CHEMISTRY [8c02f5d1-ce86-4bf5-84d5-b3496cdba6ad,83565]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2018-03-07 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update Definition
Old Value=The alkalinity of seawater is the combined negative charges due to
hydrogen carbonate and carbonate ions expressed in molal concentrations.
Alkalinity is based on seawater being electrically neutral and is
determined by titration (and therefore given the subscript t).
New Value=Refers to the capability of water to neutralize acid. This is really an expression of buffering capacity. A buffer is a solution to which an acid can be added without changing the concentration of available H+ ions (without changing the pH) appreciably.</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Water Research Center" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Refers to the capability of water to neutralize acid. This is really an expression of buffering capacity. A buffer is a solution to which an acid can be added without changing the concentration of available H+ ions (without changing the pH) appreciably.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2018-03-07 13:18:25.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="a85b812e-e4d2-4dce-bf67-d89a3e1a9122">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="b29b46ad-f05f-4144-b965-5f606ce96963"/>
    <dcterms:created>2012-06-25</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">TROPICAL OR EXTRATROPICAL CYCLONE FREQUENCY/INTENSITY</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-25 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=TROPICAL OR EXTRATROPICAL CYCLONE FREQUENCY/INTENSITY [a85b812e-e4d2-4dce-bf67-d89a3e1a9122,39661] - EXTREME WEATHER [b29b46ad-f05f-4144-b965-5f606ce96963,39651]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2024-09-23 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Refers to the frequency and intensity of tropical or extratropical cyclones. The term tropical cyclone (TC) encompasses hurricanes of the Western Hemisphere and their typhoon and cyclone equivalents elsewhere. At maturity, the TC is one of the most intense and feared storms in the world; winds exceeding 90 m s−1 (175 knots; kt) have been measured, and both its rains and storm surge can cause great loss of life and damage. The term extratropical cyclone refers to any cyclonic-scale storm that is not a tropical cyclone, usually referring only to the migratory frontal cyclones of middle and high latitudes.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="https://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Tropical_cyclone https://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Extratropical_cyclone" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Refers to the frequency and intensity of tropical or extratropical cyclones. The term tropical cyclone (TC) encompasses hurricanes of the Western Hemisphere and their typhoon and cyclone equivalents elsewhere. At maturity, the TC is one of the most intense and feared storms in the world; winds exceeding 90 m s−1 (175 knots; kt) have been measured, and both its rains and storm surge can cause great loss of life and damage. The term extratropical cyclone refers to any cyclonic-scale storm that is not a tropical cyclone, usually referring only to the migratory frontal cyclones of middle and high latitudes.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2024-09-23 10:42:55.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
