    <skos:changeNote>Date=2017-03-15 User Id=mgenazzi Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Definition added
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Dinoflagellates are protists with two flagella, a traverse flagellum typically contained in the cingulum used for motion and a longitudinal flagellum that acts as a rudder. Dinoflagellates are most common in freshwater and can be harmful as &apos;red tides&apos;.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="ucl.ac.uk" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Dinoflagellates are protists with two flagella, a traverse flagellum typically contained in the cingulum used for motion and a longitudinal flagellum that acts as a rudder. Dinoflagellates are most common in freshwater and can be harmful as &apos;red tides&apos;.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2017-03-15 11:13:40.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="ba0630cb-9a7e-4c4b-9675-c92aba7088ce">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="b1de8d2f-cfe6-4358-a4cd-5b7e19d0e585"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-08-05</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SEDIMENT CHEMISTRY</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-05 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=SEDIMENT CHEMISTRY [ba0630cb-9a7e-4c4b-9675-c92aba7088ce,105355] - SEDIMENTATION [b1de8d2f-cfe6-4358-a4cd-5b7e19d0e585,105353]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-20 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Refers to the chemical makeup and characteristics of sediments. In chemistry, sedimentation has been used to measure the size of large molecules (macromolecule), where the force of gravity is augmented with centrifugal force in an ultracentrifuge.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Refers to the chemical makeup and characteristics of sediments. In chemistry, sedimentation has been used to measure the size of large molecules (macromolecule), where the force of gravity is augmented with centrifugal force in an ultracentrifuge.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-08-20 12:48:42.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="b2a9741a-f978-46ac-83ad-e92ff07a637c">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="e6fb1b81-8ffc-486f-b1a1-2f292af8cee6"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-02-18</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">CARBONATE FORMATION</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-03-11 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The formation of carbonate, a salt of carbonic acid, characterized by the presence of the carbonate ion.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-02-18 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=CARBONATE FORMATION [b2a9741a-f978-46ac-83ad-e92ff07a637c,82337] - GEOCHEMICAL PROCESSES [e6fb1b81-8ffc-486f-b1a1-2f292af8cee6,82335]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="San Paulo University" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The formation of carbonate, a salt of carbonic acid, characterized by the presence of the carbonate ion.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-03-11 13:16:09.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="b677862b-7921-458f-a6db-0eb46469df33">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="e3b178eb-2d47-41db-aba1-43a05e9e9256"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">HYDROTHERMAL VENTS</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Pertaining to the fractures in the oceanic crust where superheated wateris ejected into the ocean, providing a source of minerals, and unusuallife forms.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="b1d65d88-7bd0-491d-91ca-4102b89dc3e7">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="b7812c71-4b9e-4016-b4ba-dfcdb7e62365"/>
    <dcterms:created>2016-06-15</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">LEAF AREA INDEX (LAI)</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2016-06-15 User Id=gee-cee Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=LEAF AREA INDEX [b1d65d88-7bd0-491d-91ca-4102b89dc3e7,188277] - VEGETATION INDEX [b7812c71-4b9e-4016-b4ba-dfcdb7e62365,84883]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2016-06-15 User Id=gee-cee Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The one sided green leaf area per unit ground area in broadleaf canopies, or as the projected needleleaf area per unit ground area in needle canopies.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2016-06-15 User Id=gee-cee Entity=PrefLabel Operation=UPDATE Field=text 
System Note=update PrefLabel
Old Value=LEAF AREA INDEX
New Value=LEAF AREA INDEX (LAI)</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="ORNL DAAC" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The one sided green leaf area per unit ground area in broadleaf canopies, or as the projected needleleaf area per unit ground area in needle canopies.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2016-06-15 08:49:43.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="e2ea5b37-7004-4943-ad69-ca39a57569a4">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="b409a30b-0e3f-4592-bec9-7d371797b4a9"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-08-06</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">FIRN FORMATION</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-06 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=FIRN FORMATION [e2ea5b37-7004-4943-ad69-ca39a57569a4,105667] - GLACIAL PROCESSES [b409a30b-0e3f-4592-bec9-7d371797b4a9,85295]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-08-26 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The formation process of firn, a partially compacted névé, which is a type of snow that has been left over from past seasons and has been recrystallized into a substance denser than névé. It is ice that is at an intermediate stage between snow and glacial ice.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="USGS" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The formation process of firn, a partially compacted névé, which is a type of snow that has been left over from past seasons and has been recrystallized into a substance denser than névé. It is ice that is at an intermediate stage between snow and glacial ice.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-08-26 09:04:02.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="b3b14df8-5197-4a26-ae61-882fdba706f3">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="b98f3a77-397d-41d7-9507-e7a3e47210b1"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">FOOD STORAGE</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Adapted from: Herren, R.V., and R.L. Donahue.  1991.  The Agriculture Dictionary, Delmar Publishers INC.  Albany, New york.   553pp. AND USDA-NAL AGRICOLA Subject Category Codes" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Any measure related to the storage of food stuffs, includingrefrigeration, canning, smoking, irradiation, drying, etc... Methods ofstorage of food products: effects of storage conditions on food quality;temperature, controlled atmosphere, etc. Shelf life.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="fceaf7e0-c22b-40a1-ba1d-37616031dba6">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="b4c7dec8-6a9a-4424-8f80-4ad1ed0bc5ec"/>
    <dcterms:created>2021-02-26</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SULFUR HEXAFLUORIDE SURFACE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-03-03 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: SF6- an inert gas categorized as hydrofluorocarbons, measured at the earth&apos;s surface. Sulfur hexafluoride absorbs thermal infrared radiation and could increase global warming as its concentration in the atmosphere increases; therefore it is a greenhouse gas.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2021-02-26 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=SULFUR HEXAFLUORIDE SURFACE [fceaf7e0-c22b-40a1-ba1d-37616031dba6,560885] - SULFUR HEXAFLUORIDE [b4c7dec8-6a9a-4424-8f80-4ad1ed0bc5ec,560883]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Agriculture, Ecosystems and Environment; v121; 30-46; 2007" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">SF6- an inert gas categorized as hydrofluorocarbons, measured at the earth&apos;s surface. Sulfur hexafluoride absorbs thermal infrared radiation and could increase global warming as its concentration in the atmosphere increases; therefore it is a greenhouse gas.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2021-03-03 09:19:36.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="cb628a66-a10b-4ef1-9261-7ce63a9439dc">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="b6164a29-8e14-4861-a30c-fefce375e284"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">JELLYFISHES</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Burn, David M. Animal World. 1980.  Octopus Books Ltd.  London." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Jellyfish are acoelomate with a body wall divided into two layers andseparated by a jelly-like mesoglea.  They are primarily radiallysymmetrical with no anus and a mouth usually surrounded by tentaclesbearing stinging nematocysts.  Jellyfish are free swimming.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="ca551e61-4b8c-46d5-8590-80cada40ebbd">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="b41894fa-2e3e-475b-b8f0-b6ffdd2d6e9c"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ANIMAL NUTRITION</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Adapted from: Herren, R.V., and R.L. Donahue.  1991.  The Agriculture Dictionary, Delmar Publishers INC.  Albany, New york.  553pp." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Any measurement related to the sum processes by which an animal utilizesthe chemical components of food through metabolism to maintain thestructural and biochemical integrity of its cells, thereby ensuring itsviability and reproductive potential.  May include natural or artificialnutrition management.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="b590bfda-a053-4439-8f86-a2811e67ce46">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="c5ff6f39-0c35-488a-96f2-f3498c678e45"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">VISIBLE RADIANCE</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Glossary of Meteorology, American Meteorological Society, 1998.&lt;BR&gt; Remote Sensing: Optics and Optical Systems, Philip N. Slatter, 1980." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">In radiometery, a measure of the intrinsic radiant intensity emitted bya radiator in a given direction. Radiance is measured in watts per squaremeter and steradian.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="e749bafe-9a0a-42cc-bed8-9b42e3e088c8">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="b41894fa-2e3e-475b-b8f0-b6ffdd2d6e9c"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ANIMAL DISEASES/DISORDERS/PESTS</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Adapted from: Herren, R.V., and R.L. Donahue.  1991.  The Agriculture Dictionary, Delmar Publishers INC.  Albany, New york.  553pp." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Measurements related to any deviation from a normal state of health inanimals which temporarily or permanently impairs vital functions.	It maybe caused by insect pests, viruses, pathogenic bacteria, parasites, poornutrition congenital or inherent deficiencies, unfavorable environment, orany combination of these, and related pests.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="b1f63bf1-a547-4189-9c7e-66a8d11facc4">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="fee25cad-7ffe-4ee2-a6f2-8116b8a0a707"/>
    <dcterms:created>2012-06-27</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">COASTAL AREAS</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-03 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: A geographical area where the land meets the sea or ocean.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-27 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=COASTAL AREAS [b1f63bf1-a547-4189-9c7e-66a8d11facc4,40251] - HUMAN SETTLEMENTS [fee25cad-7ffe-4ee2-a6f2-8116b8a0a707,40245]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="The Dictionary of Human Geography" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A geographical area where the land meets the sea or ocean.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-07-03 08:59:44.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="b296b688-0ff0-4212-9b30-30e9fe413709">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="c9e429cb-eff0-4dd3-9eca-527e0081f65c"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-03-21</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">CLOUD FRACTION</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-03-21 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: is the percentage of each pixel in satellite imagery or each gridbox in a weather or climate model that is covered with clouds. A cloud fraction of one means the pixel is completely covered with clouds, while a cloud fraction of zero represents a totally cloud free pixel.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-03-21 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=CLOUD FRACTION [b296b688-0ff0-4212-9b30-30e9fe413709,83213] - CLOUD PROPERTIES [c9e429cb-eff0-4dd3-9eca-527e0081f65c,82133]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="NASA Earth Observatory, January 2005, http://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/GlobalMaps/view.php?d1=MODAL2_M_CLD_FR" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">is the percentage of each pixel in satellite imagery or each gridbox in a weather or climate model that is covered with clouds. A cloud fraction of one means the pixel is completely covered with clouds, while a cloud fraction of zero represents a totally cloud free pixel.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-03-21 14:42:36.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="e5b724af-b661-406a-ae1f-7cd2730c0576">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="b41894fa-2e3e-475b-b8f0-b6ffdd2d6e9c"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ANIMAL MANAGEMENT SYSTEMS</skos:prefLabel>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Adapted from: Herren, R.V., and R.L. Donahue.  1991.  The Agriculture Dictionary, Delmar Publishers INC.  Albany, New york.  553pp." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Any measurement related to the science and art of the organizationand operation of animal/livestock/poultry systems so as to obtain apredetermined set of goals (e.g. profit maximization, sustainability,conservation of biodiversity, etc...). Includes, but not limited to,grazing systems, pasture systems, feedlot management, roost management,shepherding, etc...</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="d73ed320-cd5b-4994-a26a-dac5a2fc394f">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="b472632f-8e67-4892-9896-1c14c5089682"/>
    <dcterms:created>2013-02-18</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">NITRIFICATION</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-03-11 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The biological oxidation of ammonia with oxygen into nitrite followed by the oxidation of these nitrites into nitrates. Degradation of ammonia to nitrite is usually the rate limiting step of nitrification. Nitrification is an important step in the nitrogen cycle in soil.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2013-02-18 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=NITRIFICATION [d73ed320-cd5b-4994-a26a-dac5a2fc394f,82355] - BIOGEOCHEMICAL PROCESSES [b472632f-8e67-4892-9896-1c14c5089682,61437]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Collins English Dictionary" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The biological oxidation of ammonia with oxygen into nitrite followed by the oxidation of these nitrites into nitrates. Degradation of ammonia to nitrite is usually the rate limiting step of nitrification. Nitrification is an important step in the nitrogen cycle in soil.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2013-03-11 12:57:00.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="b2f12641-19c8-4b26-9496-e79da5efcb85">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="c8317644-4cb2-4e37-b536-c762f7e670ab"/>
    <dcterms:created>2012-06-26</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">RECYCLING</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-26 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=RECYCLING [b2f12641-19c8-4b26-9496-e79da5efcb85,40005] - SOCIAL BEHAVIOR [c8317644-4cb2-4e37-b536-c762f7e670ab,20567]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-29 User Id=tbs1979 Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: Involves processing used materials into new products to prevent waste of potentially useful materials, reduce the consumption of fresh raw materials, reduce energy usage, reduce air pollution, and water pollution by reducing the need for &quot;conventional&quot; waste disposal, and lower greenhouse gas emissions as compared to virgin production. Recycling is a key component of modern waste reduction.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recycling" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Involves processing used materials into new products to prevent waste of potentially useful materials, reduce the consumption of fresh raw materials, reduce energy usage, reduce air pollution, and water pollution by reducing the need for &apos;conventional&apos; waste disposal, and lower greenhouse gas emissions as compared to virgin production. Recycling is a key component of modern waste reduction.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-06-29 12:11:11.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
  <skos:Concept rdf:about="b32eb466-3fc1-4a03-8011-99002f5591cb">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="c9a13e09-98de-4ace-8d4c-b01aa04347f4"/>
    <dcterms:created>2019-07-25</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">GEOPHYSICAL HAZARDS MAPPING</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2019-07-25 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=GEOPHYSICAL HAZARDS MAPPING [b32eb466-3fc1-4a03-8011-99002f5591cb,368987] - HAZARD MAPPING [c9a13e09-98de-4ace-8d4c-b01aa04347f4,368979]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Adapted from the ​EM-DAT International Disaster Database (https://www.emdat.be/classification)." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Mapping of ​hazards originating from the solid earth. Examples include earthquakes, tsunamis, and volcanic activity.</skos:definition>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
