  <skos:Concept rdf:about="caddaef6-1a60-490a-938e-9107885f286f">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="b887d3e5-4280-43d2-a34e-0f63ac086b6a"/>
    <dcterms:created>2012-06-25</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">MULTIVARIATE ENSO INDEX</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2020-02-14 User Id=tstevens Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=DELETE 
User Note=Remove Concepts
System Note=delete narrower relation
Old Value=MULTIVARIATE ENSO INDEX [caddaef6-1a60-490a-938e-9107885f286f,86851] - MEI [087bffd1-f4f5-49ef-98d8-8e7d1169bfe8,86853]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-09 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The MEI is a leading indicator for the El Niño/Southern Oscillation (ENSO) phenomenon and is calculated as the first unrotated Principal Component of six observed variables over the tropical Pacific Ocean. These six variables are: sea level pressure, zonal and meridional components of the surface wind, sea surface temperature, surface air temperature, and total cloudiness fraction of the sky. The MEI is computed for each of twelve sliding bimonthly seasons (Dec/Jan, Jan/Feb,..., Nov/Dec). Negative values of the MEI represent the cold ENSO phase (La Niña), while positive MEI values represent the warm ENSO phase (El Niño).
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-25 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=MULTIVARIATE ENSO INDEX [caddaef6-1a60-490a-938e-9107885f286f,39693] - TELECONNECTIONS [b887d3e5-4280-43d2-a34e-0f63ac086b6a,20219]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-25 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add narrower relation
New Value=MULTIVARIATE ENSO INDEX [caddaef6-1a60-490a-938e-9107885f286f,39693] - MEI [087bffd1-f4f5-49ef-98d8-8e7d1169bfe8,39695]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Physical Sciences Division, Earth System Research Laboratory, NOAA:  http://www.esrl.noaa.gov/psd/enso/mei/" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The MEI is a leading indicator for the El Niño/Southern Oscillation (ENSO) phenomenon and is calculated as the first unrotated Principal Component of six observed variables over the tropical Pacific Ocean. These six variables are: sea level pressure, zonal and meridional components of the surface wind, sea surface temperature, surface air temperature, and total cloudiness fraction of the sky. The MEI is computed for each of twelve sliding bimonthly seasons (Dec/Jan, Jan/Feb,..., Nov/Dec). Negative values of the MEI represent the cold ENSO phase (La Niña), while positive MEI values represent the warm ENSO phase (El Niño).</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2020-02-14 15:07:37.0</dcterms:modified>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
  </skos:Concept>
