    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">WEST PACIFIC INDEX</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-25 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add narrower relation
New Value=WEST PACIFIC INDEX [bdb6eafa-f4e1-4536-b513-4c787f829722,22997] - WP [fe25c265-296d-4671-9e66-9b752b78bb60,39699]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-09 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The WP pattern is a primary mode of low-frequency variability over the North Pacific in all months, and has been previously described by both Barnston and Livezey (1987) and Wallace and Gutzler (1981). During winter and spring, the pattern consists of a north-south dipole of anomalies, with one center located over the Kamchatka Peninsula and another broad center of opposite sign covering portions of southeastern Asia and the low latitudes of the extreme western North Pacific. Therefore, strong positive or negative phases of this pattern reflect pronounced zonal and meridional variations in the location and intensity of the entrance region of the Pacific (or East Asian) jet steam. 

In the summer and fall, the WP pattern becomes increasingly wave-like, and a third prominent center appears over Alaska and the Beaufort Sea, with a sign opposite to the center over the western North Pacific. This wave structure is most evident in the Fall, when it extends downstream along a quasi great-circle route into the western United States. The time series of the WP pattern indicates considerable intermonthly and interannual variability, and persistence of a particular phase of the pattern is relatively common.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2020-02-18 User Id=tstevens Entity=NarrowerRelation Operation=DELETE 
User Note=Remove Concepts
System Note=delete narrower relation
Old Value=WEST PACIFIC INDEX [bdb6eafa-f4e1-4536-b513-4c787f829722,86447] - WP [fe25c265-296d-4671-9e66-9b752b78bb60,86857]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="Source: NOAA Climate Prediction Center, http://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The WP pattern is a primary mode of low-frequency variability over the North Pacific in all months, and has been previously described by both Barnston and Livezey (1987) and Wallace and Gutzler (1981). During winter and spring, the pattern consists of a north-south dipole of anomalies, with one center located over the Kamchatka Peninsula and another broad center of opposite sign covering portions of southeastern Asia and the low latitudes of the extreme western North Pacific. Therefore, strong positive or negative phases of this pattern reflect pronounced zonal and meridional variations in the location and intensity of the entrance region of the Pacific (or East Asian) jet steam. In the summer and fall, the WP pattern becomes increasingly wave-like, and a third prominent center appears over Alaska and the Beaufort Sea, with a sign opposite to the center over the western North Pacific. This wave structure is most evident in the Fall, when it extends downstream along a quasi great-circle route into the western United States. The time series of the WP pattern indicates considerable intermonthly and interannual variability, and persistence of a particular phase of the pattern is relatively common.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2020-02-18 09:03:16.0</dcterms:modified>
