    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">PACIFIC WARM POOL</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-09-14 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=PACIFIC WARM POOL [db1000b8-3b19-46fa-9d79-379379d654ac,40581] - SEA SURFACE TEMPERATURE INDICES [b83895e9-bac8-49fe-bcf5-8fe4d8fcaa16,39747]</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-09-14 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The Indian Ocean/West Pacific Warm Pool extends almost half way around the globe, stretching along the equator south of India, through the waters off Sumatra, Java, Borneo, and New Guinea, and into the central Pacific Ocean. The waters of the Warm Pool are warmer than any other open ocean on Earth. Because these waters are hot enough to drive heat and moisture high into the atmosphere, the warm pool has a large effect on the climate of surrounding lands. In fact, the slow fluctuations of size and intensity of the warm pool may be linked with the intensity of El Niño.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="NASA Earth Observatory" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The Indian Ocean/West Pacific Warm Pool extends almost half way around the globe, stretching along the equator south of India, through the waters off Sumatra, Java, Borneo, and New Guinea, and into the central Pacific Ocean. The waters of the Warm Pool are warmer than any other open ocean on Earth. Because these waters are hot enough to drive heat and moisture high into the atmosphere, the warm pool has a large effect on the climate of surrounding lands. In fact, the slow fluctuations of size and intensity of the warm pool may be linked with the intensity of El Niño.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-09-14 13:53:50.0</dcterms:modified>
