    <dcterms:created>2012-06-26</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">FREEZING INDEX</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-07-09 User Id=mpmorahan Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The total annual freezing and thawing indices are defined as the cumulative number of degree-days when air temperatures are below and above zero degrees Celsius. The total annual freezing index has been widely used to predict permafrost distribution; estimate the maximum thickness of sea, lake, and river ice, and the maximum depth of ground-frost penetration; and classify snow types. The annual total thawing index has been used to predict permafrost distribution and to estimate the maximum depth of thaw in frozen ground. Both total freezing and thawing indices are important parameters for engineering design in cold regions.
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2012-06-26 User Id=gee-cee Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=FREEZING INDEX [bc6d73a9-4943-4a3e-9f9d-9406fa54b0bc,39795] - TEMPERATURE INDICES [e8580cbb-701a-4ab1-a40f-5fae4ae1ea24,39791]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="NSIDC:  http://nsidc.org/data/NSIDC-0063.html  Zhang, T. 1998. Global Annual Freezing and Thawing Indices. Boulder, CO, USA: National Snow and Ice Data Center. Digital media." xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The total annual freezing and thawing indices are defined as the cumulative number of degree-days when air temperatures are below and above zero degrees Celsius. The total annual freezing index has been widely used to predict permafrost distribution; estimate the maximum thickness of sea, lake, and river ice, and the maximum depth of ground-frost penetration; and classify snow types. The annual total thawing index has been used to predict permafrost distribution and to estimate the maximum depth of thaw in frozen ground. Both total freezing and thawing indices are important parameters for engineering design in cold regions.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2012-07-09 15:00:01.0</dcterms:modified>
