  <skos:Concept rdf:about="cb4d21ab-70a8-4111-be0a-e5508b256110">
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="d2f152e1-7398-44ad-b0a4-77ee6ef5bd6c"/>
    <dcterms:created>2024-02-28</dcterms:created>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ABSOLUTE ABUNDANCE</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2024-02-28 User Id=tstevens Entity=Definition Operation=INSERT 
System Note=insert Definition
New Value=id: null
text: The quantification of the precise amount of a specific DNA or RNA molecule in a sample. Absolute abundance provides the quantitative abundance of DNA or RNA in a sample, as opposed to the relative abundance produced by traditional PCR methods. Two common methods to determine absolute abundance include:
Cell-based methods such as flow cytometry used in ddPCR
Molecular methods used in qPCR
language code: en</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote>Date=2024-02-28 User Id=tstevens Entity=BroaderRelation Operation=INSERT 
User Note=Insert Concept
System Note=add broader relation
New Value=ABSOLUTE ABUNDANCE [cb4d21ab-70a8-4111-be0a-e5508b256110,1603383] - OMICS METHODOLOGY [d2f152e1-7398-44ad-b0a4-77ee6ef5bd6c,1603367]</skos:changeNote>
    <gcmd:reference gcmd:text="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6581686/" xml:lang="en"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The quantification of the precise amount of a specific DNA or RNA molecule in a sample. Absolute abundance provides the quantitative abundance of DNA or RNA in a sample, as opposed to the relative abundance produced by traditional PCR methods. Two common methods to determine absolute abundance include:Cell-based methods such as flow cytometry used in ddPCRMolecular methods used in qPCR</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:modified>2024-02-28 14:49:38.0</dcterms:modified>
